## Why This Is Asked

Tests 3-valued propagation in a practical policy setting, ensuring candidates handle unknowns in real constraint systems.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene logic: T>U>F; ¬ maps T↔F, ¬U=U; ∨/∧ use max/min semantics.
- Constraint propagation with backtracking to avoid F conclusions.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal Kleene implication sketch (for illustration)
function kleeneImp(A,B){
  const not = v => (v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U');
  // simplistic placeholder: avoid F when A is not definitively true
  return (A==='T' && B==='F') ? 'F' : 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to detect unsatisfiability with remaining U values?
- How does variable ordering affect worst-case exploration?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in 3-valued logic, custom connectives, and constraint propagation under backtracking. The new stable connective S introduces nontrivial propagation behavior while keeping Kleene semantics intuitive.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic with A→B = ¬A ∨ B
- Custom unary operator S(X) mapping {T,F,U} → {T,F,U}
- Constraint propagation with max/min for ∨/∧ and handling of U

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(x){return x==='T'?'F':x==='F'?'T':'U';}
function or(a,b){return (a==='T'||b==='T')?'T':(a==='U'||b==='U')?'U':'F';}
function and(a,b){return (a==='F'||b==='F')?'F':(a==='U'||b==='U')?'U':'T';}
function S(x){return x==='T'?'T':x==='F'?'F':'U';}
```

