## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new, well-defined operator (Maj) within Kleene-like three-valued logic and requires concrete CSP reasoning with backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- a custom 3-ary Maj operator (majority with T/F bias)
- constraint propagation and backtracking in non-binary domains

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function maj(a,b,c){
  const t=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='T').length;
  const f=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='F').length;
  if(t>=2) return 'T';
  if(f>=2) return 'F';
  return 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend Maj to 4 inputs while preserving monotonicity?
- What is the impact of introducing additional unknowns on backtracking complexity?

## Why This Is Asked

Temporal-like constraints on feature flags across release cycles test three-valued propagation and branching heuristics—skills essential for configuration validation at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic with implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Propagation rules for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Backtracking search treating U as a first-class domain value

## Code Example

```javascript
// Three-valued backtracking propagator sketch
const VALUES = { T: 2, U: 1, F: 0 };
const NOT = { T: 'F', F: 'T', U: 'U' };
const OR = (a, b) => VALUES[a] >= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const AND = (a, b) => VALUES[a] <= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const IMPLIES = (a, b) => OR(NOT[a], b);

function propagate(constraints, assignment) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [ante, conseq] of constraints) {
      const a = assignment[ante], c = assignment[conseq];
      if (a === 'F' && c !== 'F') { assignment[conseq] = 'F'; changed = true; }
      if (a === 'T' && c !== 'T') { assignment[conseq] = 'T'; changed = true; }
      if (c === 'F' && a !== 'F') { assignment[ante] = 'F'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return assignment;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How does the branching heuristic affect worst-case complexity?
- How would you extend this to N time steps?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and analyze dynamic constraint propagation with 3-valued domains, going beyond static logic. The problem requires constructing a concrete propagation trace and a fixed point that preserves an unknown value, mirroring real-world policy engines.

## Key Concepts
- Forward-propagation with 3-valued domains
- Fixed-point convergence
- Handling U without monotone destruction
- Traceable propagation: demonstrate steps and termination

## Code Example
```javascript
function applyRules(state){
  // state: {X1,X2,X3,X4} values in {T,F,U}
  // R1
  if(state.X1==="T" && state.X2==="T") state.X3="T";
  // R2
  if(state.X4==="F") state.X1="F";
  // R3
  if(state.X3==="F") {
    if(state.X2!=="F" && state.X4!=="F") { /* no change */ }
  }
  // R4
  if(state.X1==="U" || state.X2==="U") state.X4="U";
  return state;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect non-termination or oscillation in this system?
- How would you extend to ensure a fixed point with at least one U is reachable from any initial state?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply paraconsistent LP semantics to CSP-style constraint solving, including constructing a concrete model and a propagation strategy.

## Key Concepts

- Priest's Logic of Paradox (LP)
- Paraconsistent 3-valued truth tables for ∨, ∧, ¬, →
- CSP backtracking with forward checking under LP
- Concrete satisfying assignment with at least one B

## Code Example

```javascript
// LP semantics for 3-valued logic (T > F, B = both)
function lpNot(v){ return v=== 'T' ? 'F' : v==='F' ? 'T' : 'B';}
function lpOr(a,b){ if(a==='T' || b==='T') return 'T'; if(a==='B' || b==='B') return 'B'; return 'F';}
function lpAnd(a,b){ if(a==='F' || b==='F') return 'F'; if(a==='T' && b==='T') return 'T'; return 'B';}
function lpImp(a,b){ // a -> b = (¬a) ∨ b
  return lpOr(lpNot(a), b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to detect unsatisfiable constraint sets efficiently in LP?
- How would you scale this to hundreds of variables with incremental propagation?
